1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:10,075
High above our planet, a laboratory the size of a football field circles Earth every ninety minutes. That is the International Space Station.

2
00:00:10,575 --> 00:00:16,975
Traveling at about five miles per second, it laps the globe in the time it takes to watch a feature film.

3
00:00:17,475 --> 00:00:25,000
The station flies about two hundred and fifty miles above the surface at seventeen thousand five hundred miles per hour.

4
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:32,500
Sixteen times each day, the station laps the planet, and from its cupola, Earth looks like a living painting.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
It is a home in orbit, continuously occupied since the year two thousand.

6
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:45,075
More than two hundred and fifty astronauts have lived and worked aboard, representing nearly thirty countries.

7
00:00:45,575 --> 00:00:54,550
Every ninety minutes, the view outside the cupola flips from blazing daylight to the thin blue line of the atmosphere against the black of space.

8
00:00:55,050 --> 00:01:03,275
Astronauts watch the sun rise and set in rapid succession, sixteen sunrises and sixteen sunsets every single day.

9
00:01:03,775 --> 00:01:07,450
It is a rhythm no one on the ground experiences.

10
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:15,375
The protective shell of our atmosphere looks startlingly thin from up there — a fragile blue line between life and the void.

11
00:01:15,875 --> 00:01:21,575
You can see the curve of the planet, the thin veil of air, and the endless ocean all at once.

12
00:01:22,075 --> 00:01:34,700
The cupola is the station's window to the world. It is a dome built for observation, with seven windows that look out onto Earth, onto spacewalks, and onto arriving and departing spacecraft.

13
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:40,300
Those seven windows give the crew a panoramic view few people will ever have.

14
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:46,050
The cupola was delivered with the Tranquility node on February ninth, twenty-ten.

15
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:53,050
From there, the crew guide robotics operations, watch spacecraft dock, and photograph our world.

16
00:01:53,550 --> 00:02:01,325
The cupola supports robotics operations, docking, and photography — a workstation for observing Earth and the vehicle.

17
00:02:01,825 --> 00:02:08,400
Station crew members frequently point their cameras outside the cupola to photograph the planet below.

18
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:16,325
Over twenty years, cupola photography has built one of the longest continuous visual records of Earth ever made.

19
00:02:16,825 --> 00:02:25,550
Through those windows, astronauts watch hurricanes spin, volcanoes smoke, city lights bloom at night, and glaciers creep across the land.

20
00:02:26,050 --> 00:02:31,900
This is Earth observation from orbit, and it helps scientists understand our changing planet.

21
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:40,900
Each image, each observation, adds to a record of Earth that stretches back decades and helps us track how the planet is changing.

22
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:50,125
Astronauts can use the cupola's robotics workstation to command the Canadarm2 to reach out, grapple, and install spacecraft.

23
00:02:50,625 --> 00:02:57,650
The data feeds climate models, disaster response, and scientists studying forests, farms, and coastlines.

24
00:02:58,150 --> 00:03:07,125
The station is more than a factory in the sky. For about a quarter of a century, it has been a classroom, a laboratory, and a proving ground.

25
00:03:07,625 --> 00:03:15,800
Orbiting our planet while hosting nearly three hundred astronauts from across the globe, the crew have carried out thousands of experiments.

26
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:25,925
The research done here — in human health, in materials, in Earth science — prepares humanity for longer stays in space and for missions farther from home.

27
00:03:26,425 --> 00:03:36,400
It is an enduring platform for international cooperation in orbit, a fragile framework of modules grown into the most enduring platform humans have built.

28
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:43,750
Experiments on the station have yielded advances in medicine, materials science, and climate resilience.

29
00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:56,150
Astronauts who spend months aboard the station describe something called the overview effect. Seeing Earth without borders, a single fragile sphere hanging in the void, changes how they see home.

30
00:03:56,650 --> 00:04:02,950
National boundaries disappear from this height. What remains is one planet, shared by everyone.

31
00:04:03,450 --> 00:04:07,250
From orbit, Earth is not a collection of separate places.

32
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:16,125
From this height there are no lines drawn on the surface. There is only land and ocean, cloud and ice, one interconnected system.

33
00:04:16,625 --> 00:04:22,450
The atmosphere is a thin protective shell, a fragile blue line between life and the void.

34
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:26,625
It reminds us how small and precious our home really is.

35
00:04:27,125 --> 00:04:37,875
The station's continuous presence in orbit — people living and working in space, day after day — reminds us that we are capable of looking after our home when we choose to.

36
00:04:38,375 --> 00:04:45,625
Two hundred and fifty miles up. Seventeen thousand five hundred miles per hour. Seven windows on the world.

37
00:04:46,125 --> 00:04:57,050
The International Space Station proves that, seen from orbit, Earth is not a collection of separate places — it is one home, shared by everyone who has ever lived.
